I decided to start a website hosting business on a VPS.

I set up Cloudflare as a frontend proxy for security and to speed up asset delivery.

To serve the websites, I used Nginx with server_name blocks for each domain:
server {
	listen 80;
	server_name example.com;
	root /var/www/example.com;
}

To serve PHP, I spun up a PHP-FPM pool. I used Unix socket connection to Nginx rather than TCP/IP.

To make things easy for myself, I created a single FTP user with access to /var/www for uploading files. This means all sites used the same permissions.

I also used the same paths for including files in all sites:
include_once("/var/www/common/lib.php");

Within the common code, I used $_GET['secure_file'] to load some images. Since there was no validation, I could upload a PHP file to common/ and specify its path in secure_file.

Now anytime someone visited any of my sites, my uploaded PHP file would run, giving me remote code execution on the VPS!

